WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.877
Meet The Economics of Everything: an interactive economics lab built around everyday decisions.

00:00:06.877 --> 00:00:10.206
In this chapter, you are the concert promoter.

00:00:10.206 --> 00:00:14.622
Your mission is to put on a great night without losing money.

00:00:14.622 --> 00:00:19.255
This is a playable model, not a ticket shop or a sales forecast.

00:00:19.255 --> 00:00:23.381
Change an assumption, see the consequences, then ask why.

00:00:23.531 --> 00:00:25.538
Start in Mix Your Margins.

00:00:25.538 --> 00:00:29.554
Drag the ticket-price fader or type an exact number.

00:00:29.554 --> 00:00:32.411
The second fader sets venue capacity.

00:00:32.411 --> 00:00:39.825
The animated dance floor and five signs turn your inputs into guests, revenue, costs and profit.

00:00:39.825 --> 00:00:47.933
At twenty dollars, our example has a hundred and fifty guests and seven hundred and fifty dollars profit.

00:00:48.083 --> 00:00:50.762
Now raise the price to thirty dollars.

00:00:50.762 --> 00:00:55.344
In this model, fewer people buy: attendance falls to one hundred.

00:00:55.344 --> 00:01:02.323
Revenue stays at three thousand dollars, but per-guest costs fall, so profit rises to one thousand.

00:01:02.323 --> 00:01:04.790
That is price elasticity in action.

00:01:04.790 --> 00:01:09.513
A bigger crowd and a better business are not always the same thing.

00:01:09.663 --> 00:01:15.006
Next, return the price to twenty and increase capacity to one thousand.

00:01:15.006 --> 00:01:18.542
You still have only a hundred and fifty buyers.

00:01:18.542 --> 00:01:24.261
Venue costs rise, and profit becomes a twelve-hundred-and-fifty-dollar loss.

00:01:24.261 --> 00:01:27.948
Capacity is a constraint, not a demand generator.

00:01:27.948 --> 00:01:34.344
Use this lever when testing the cost of expanding before assuming the room will fill.

00:01:34.494 --> 00:01:38.212
Open the cost breakdown to see where the money goes.

00:01:38.212 --> 00:01:45.148
In Add a variable, expose stage and sound, venue costs, cost per guest, promotion or sponsorship.

00:01:45.148 --> 00:01:47.436
Change one assumption at a time.

00:01:47.436 --> 00:01:51.655
Fixed and variable costs explain why revenue is not profit.

00:01:51.655 --> 00:01:56.946
The break-even message helps you see what must change to cover your costs.

00:01:57.096 --> 00:02:03.273
Use the Audience selector to choose price-based demand or set attendance yourself.

00:02:03.273 --> 00:02:06.512
Venue setup loads illustrative assumptions.

00:02:06.512 --> 00:02:13.969
Promotion spending and its assumed audience boost are separate: spending does not guarantee buyers.

00:02:13.969 --> 00:02:20.070
These controls let you test uncertainty, rather than disguise it as a prediction.

00:02:20.070 --> 00:02:23.309
All financial amounts remain in US dollars.

00:02:23.459 --> 00:02:27.422
Before choosing your date, open Who else is on the bill.

00:02:27.422 --> 00:02:33.436
Pick Atlanta, Berlin or Kingston, Jamaica, choose a date, then press Check this date.

00:02:33.436 --> 00:02:39.734
Simulation mode uses clearly labeled made-up events; live mode requests sourced research.

00:02:39.734 --> 00:02:43.838
This introduces competition for scarce audience attention.

00:02:43.838 --> 00:02:50.277
Listings do not automatically change attendance: decide which assumptions you want to test.

00:02:50.427 --> 00:02:53.314
Save this mix before your next experiment.

00:02:53.314 --> 00:02:57.714
Then change one input and compare the saved and current results.

00:02:57.714 --> 00:03:03.558
Restore the saved mix to return to your baseline, or reset the concert to start over.

00:03:03.558 --> 00:03:13.389
This is the economic habit we want to teach: compare alternatives, make assumptions visible, and understand what you give up for what you gain.

00:03:13.539 --> 00:03:17.010
When a result surprises you, ask your concert coach.

00:03:17.010 --> 00:03:23.884
Type a question, choose a suggested question, or use the microphone, review the transcript and send it.

00:03:23.884 --> 00:03:26.554
Enable Speak replies to hear the answer.

00:03:26.554 --> 00:03:34.830
The coach explains this bounded concert model using your current scenario, helping connect the numbers to the economic idea.

00:03:34.980 --> 00:03:40.525
For the builders in the room: shared deterministic formulas own the money.

00:03:40.525 --> 00:03:43.822
Blender assets and Babylon render the world.

00:03:43.822 --> 00:03:54.313
One n8n workflow handles coaching and event research; TypeSafe AI classifies concert questions, and the coach selects approved explanations.

00:03:54.313 --> 00:03:59.634
AI helps interpret the model; it does not invent the financial results.

00:03:59.634 --> 00:04:03.980
That separation makes the learning experience inspectable.

00:04:04.130 --> 00:04:10.998
The Economics of Everything starts with a familiar experience and makes its hidden economics visible.

00:04:10.998 --> 00:04:19.021
The concert is the working chapter: demand, constraints, costs and trade-offs you can manipulate, not just read about.

00:04:19.021 --> 00:04:20.925
Try your first decision now.

00:04:20.925 --> 00:04:24.461
Move the price fader, watch the result, and ask why.

00:04:24.461 --> 00:04:27.589
The outcome is not a perfect concert forecast.

00:04:27.589 --> 00:04:29.833
It is a better economic question.
